      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I completely understand your frustration. Yes, there are ways to force pandas to behave more predictably when it comes to slicing and assigning values.\n\nOne approach is to use the `.copy()` method explicitly when creating a slice. This will always create a copy, regardless of the memory layout or cache flags. So, instead of doing `df[slice] = value`, you can do `df[slice].copy() = value`. This ensures that the assignment will always propagate back to the original DataFrame's memory address.\n\nAnother approach is to use the `.at` or `.iat` methods instead of assignment. These methods allow you to access and modify specific elements of the DataFrame without creating a slice. For example, `df.at[row, column] = value` will always create a view, and modifying it will propagate back to the original DataFrame's memory address.\n\nYou can also use the `.loc` and `.iloc` methods to access and modify specific elements of the DataFrame. These methods are similar to `.at` and `.iat`, but they allow you to access and modify entire rows or columns. For example, `df.loc[row] = value` will always create a view, and modifying it will propagate back to the original DataFrame's memory address",
